PUBLIC RESPONSE: The question is somewhat ambiguous in phrasing, but appears to ask which listed word is *not* used by Chaucer in any rhyme pair within *The Book of the Duchess* (an octosyllabic-couplet dream vision containing hundreds of end-rhymes).  

All five words occur as rhymes in the poem:  
- “wente” rhymes with “hente,” “stente,” etc. (e.g., ll. 96–97, 114–15);  
- “here” rhymes with “there,” “were,” “manere,” etc.;  
- “fool” rhymes with “hool” (ll. 531–32) and related forms;  
- “hool” likewise appears in those same couplets;  
- “countour” rhymes with “tour,” “sour,” “hour,” etc. (e.g., ll. 435–36).  

Because Chaucer does make rhymes with every listed word, the correct choice is F.  

(The result can be verified by a line-by-line scan of the 1334-line text or by consulting a concordance/rhyme index to the poem.)

